# {{projectName}} - NestJS Project Rules

## General Guidelines
- Use TypeScript for all files
- Follow NestJS best practices and conventions
- Use dependency injection and decorators properly
- Write clean, readable, and maintainable code

## Naming Conventions
### Module Files
- `user.module.ts` - Module definition
- `user.resolver.ts` - GraphQL resolver{{#unless isGraphQL}} (not applicable for this project){{/unless}}
- `user.controller.ts` - REST controller{{#unless isRest}} (not applicable for this project){{/unless}}
- `user.service.ts` - Business logic service
- `user.repository.ts` - Data access layer{{#if isMongoose}} using Mongoose{{/if}}{{#if isPrisma}} using Prisma{{/if}}
- `user.schema.ts` - Database schema{{#if isMongoose}} (Mongoose){{/if}}
- `user.input.ts` - GraphQL input types{{#unless isGraphQL}} (not applicable for this project){{/unless}}
- `user.object.ts` - GraphQL object types{{#unless isGraphQL}} (not applicable for this project){{/unless}}
- `user.dto.ts` - Data transfer objects{{#unless isRest}} (not applicable for this project){{/unless}}

### Query/Mutation Naming{{#if isGraphQL}}
- `user` - get single user by fields
- `users` - get multiple users with filters
- `userCreate` - create a new user
- `userUpdate` - update an existing user
- `userDelete` - delete a user
- `userDeleteMany` - delete multiple users{{/if}}

## Project Structure
```
src/
├── config/                 # Configuration modules with validation
├── common/                # Shared modules and utilities
│   └── modules/          # Common modules (DB, GraphQL, etc.)
├── example-module/       # Example feature module
│   ├── schemas/         # Database schemas
│   ├── objects/         # GraphQL objects{{#unless isGraphQL}} (not applicable){{/unless}}
│   ├── inputs/          # GraphQL inputs{{#unless isGraphQL}} (not applicable){{/unless}}
│   ├── dto/             # REST DTOs{{#unless isRest}} (not applicable){{/unless}}
│   ├── services/        # Business logic
│   ├── repositories/    # Data access
│   ├── resolvers/       # GraphQL resolvers{{#unless isGraphQL}} (not applicable){{/unless}}
│   ├── controllers/     # REST controllers{{#unless isRest}} (not applicable){{/unless}}
│   └── errors/          # Custom errors
└── main.ts              # Application entry point
```

## Configuration
- Use class-validator for environment variable validation
- Keep sensitive data in environment variables
- Use proper typing with ConfigService<EnvironmentVariables>

{{#if isMongoose}}
## Database (MongoDB with Mongoose)
- Define schemas in `schemas/` directory
- Use proper decorators (@Prop, @Schema)
- Register all schemas in `app-mongoose-models.module.ts`
- Use repositories for data access, never access models directly in services
- Use `@InjectModel(Model.name)` for dependency injection

### Repository Pattern
```typescript
constructor(@InjectModel(User.name) private readonly userModel: Model<UserDocument>) {}
```
{{/if}}

{{#if isPrisma}}
## Database ({{#if_eq database 'postgresql'}}PostgreSQL{{/if_eq}}{{#if_eq database 'mysql'}}MySQL{{/if_eq}} with Prisma)
- Define schemas in `prisma/schema.prisma`
- Use PrismaService for database operations
- Run `npm run prisma:generate` after schema changes
- Use transactions for complex operations
{{/if}}

{{#if isGraphQL}}
## GraphQL Best Practices
- Use GraphQL objects (`*.object.ts`) for return types
- Use GraphQL inputs (`*.input.ts`) for arguments
- Implement proper pagination using `PaginationInput`
- Use GraphQL scalars from `graphql-scalars` package
- Object constructors should handle both Document and Object types:

```typescript
constructor(input: UserDocument | UserObject) {
  Object.assign(this, {
    ...((input as any)._doc
      ? (input as UserDocument).toObject({ virtuals: true })
      : input),
  });
}
```
{{/if}}

{{#if isRest}}
## REST API Best Practices
- Use DTOs for request/response validation
- Apply Swagger decorators for API documentation
- Use class-validator for input validation
- Follow RESTful naming conventions
- Use proper HTTP status codes
{{/if}}

## Error Handling
- Create custom error classes extending base error types
- Use proper error codes for frontend integration
- Register error enums in `registerEnums.ts`

{{#if hasRedis}}
## Redis
- Use RedisService for all cache operations
- Implement proper error handling for cache failures
- Set appropriate TTL values
{{/if}}

## Testing
- Write unit tests for services and repositories
- Write integration tests for resolvers/controllers
- Mock external dependencies
- Use test databases for integration tests

## Code Quality
- Use ESLint and Prettier for code formatting
- Follow TypeScript strict mode
- Use meaningful variable and function names
- Write JSDoc comments for complex functions
- Keep functions small and focused
